1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,769
Hi, we’re Sumit, Priyamvadha and Amiya.

2
00:00:02,769 --> 00:00:11,384
Our question is simple to say: can the answers adults give on a health survey, things like blood pressure, weight and how healthy they feel,

3
00:00:11,384 --> 00:00:17,538
pick out the people who have diabetes or prediabetes, while missing as few of them as possible?

4
00:00:17,538 --> 00:00:22,769
We used the CDC’s 2015 BRFSS survey, about a quarter of a million adults.

5
00:00:22,769 --> 00:00:32,307
In the next nine minutes: the data, how we kept the evaluation honest, the models, the screening cutoff we locked before we ever looked at the test set,

6
00:00:32,307 --> 00:00:35,999
and the final results, including who the model misses.

7
00:00:35,999 --> 00:00:45,230
A questionnaire costs almost nothing, so if it can tell us who should get a blood test, screening can reach people who would never otherwise be tested.

8
00:00:45,230 --> 00:00:52,922
We had about 254,000 adults, each with 21 answers: blood pressure, cholesterol, BMI, how healthy they feel, age, income and so on.

9
00:00:52,922 --> 00:00:55,999
Just under 16% report diabetes or prediabetes.

10
00:00:55,999 --> 00:01:03,076
We treat a missed case as the expensive mistake, so our rule is: catch a fixed share of cases, 80%,

11
00:01:03,076 --> 00:01:08,614
and then compare approaches by how many people they have to flag to get there.

12
00:01:08,614 --> 00:01:15,383
And the comparison that matters is not against guessing; it’s against the paper risk test clinics already hand out.

13
00:01:15,383 --> 00:01:23,075
The data come from Kaggle’s cleaned version of the 2015 BRFSS. We used the three-class file, because it separates prediabetes from diabetes.

14
00:01:23,075 --> 00:01:27,383
Our main outcome counts both as positive: that’s 15.8% of people.

15
00:01:27,383 --> 00:01:35,691
The roadmap mentioned about 14%; that turns out to be diabetes alone, and we checked that Kaggle’s binary file actually codes prediabetes as negative.

16
00:01:35,691 --> 00:01:41,845
We think prediabetes belongs in the positive group, because it’s exactly what screening is meant to catch.

17
00:01:41,845 --> 00:01:47,383
We also ran a harder sensitivity analysis on prediabetes alone, where only 2% are positive.

18
00:01:47,383 --> 00:01:54,152
One more thing: there were no missing or invalid values, and we kept everyone, including people with identical answers,

19
00:01:54,152 --> 00:01:57,537
because there’s no ID to prove they’re duplicates.

20
00:01:57,537 --> 00:02:05,229
Exploration and the hypothesis tests point to the same five answers: general health, high blood pressure, BMI, difficulty walking and high cholesterol.

21
00:02:05,229 --> 00:02:14,152
All 25 of our planned tests were significant, but with a quarter of a million people that tells you very little, so we read effect sizes.

22
00:02:14,152 --> 00:02:18,152
High blood pressure goes with almost five times the odds.

23
00:02:18,152 --> 00:02:25,844
The biggest odds ratio, though, is for having had a cholesterol check, and that’s a warning: it mostly measures contact with doctors,

24
00:02:25,844 --> 00:02:28,921
and doctors are who make the diagnosis.

25
00:02:28,921 --> 00:02:35,998
These are also one-answer-at-a-time associations; later we’ll see several of them shrink or even reverse when everything is considered together.

26
00:02:35,998 --> 00:02:43,690
Before modeling we fixed one split for the whole team: 80% for training, divided into five fixed folds, and 20% locked away.

27
00:02:43,690 --> 00:02:51,382
The code literally refuses to hand out the test rows until a saved threshold exists for that exact split. Three checks here.

28
00:02:51,382 --> 00:02:56,613
Scaling happens inside the model pipelines, so it only ever learns from training folds.

29
00:02:56,613 --> 00:03:02,459
About 15% of people share their exact answers with someone else; we tested whether that leaks,

30
00:03:02,459 --> 00:03:07,382
and keeping those people together changed the score by a thousandth at most.

31
00:03:07,382 --> 00:03:15,074
And class weighting, the usual fix for imbalance, didn’t rank people any better; it just inflated everyone’s risk from 16% to 39%.

32
00:03:15,074 --> 00:03:19,074
So we kept honest probabilities and chose the cutoff ourselves.

33
00:03:19,074 --> 00:03:27,689
We tuned three models: logistic regression as the interpretable baseline, a random forest, and XGBoost, all on the same five folds and scored by PR-AUC,

34
00:03:27,689 --> 00:03:36,304
which suits a rare outcome. The big jump is from the paper checklist to any model: PR-AUC goes from 0.33 to about 0.44 to 0.46.

35
00:03:36,304 --> 00:03:42,458
To catch 80% of cases, the checklist flags half of everyone; the models flag 38 to 39%.

36
00:03:42,458 --> 00:03:49,227
We wrote the selection rule down before the run: take the best model unless logistic regression isn’t significantly worse.

37
00:03:49,227 --> 00:03:53,842
XGBoost won in all five folds against both, so it went forward.

38
00:03:53,842 --> 00:04:01,842
But notice how close logistic regression is; if you need a model you can explain line by line, it gives up very little.

39
00:04:01,842 --> 00:04:04,611
What do the models actually use?

40
00:04:04,611 --> 00:04:11,688
Shuffling one answer at a time and measuring the drop in performance, all three models agree: BMI, general health, age,

41
00:04:11,688 --> 00:04:17,226
high blood pressure and high cholesterol. Everything else barely matters once those five are known.

42
00:04:17,226 --> 00:04:23,072
We also fitted one ordinary logistic regression with all 21 answers to get adjusted odds ratios,

43
00:04:23,072 --> 00:04:27,072
and that’s a useful warning about the earlier one-at-a-time numbers.

44
00:04:27,072 --> 00:04:33,841
Bad physical-health days looked risky on their own, but the effect reverses once general health is in the model.

45
00:04:33,841 --> 00:04:40,303
Smoking’s association disappears, because smokers are older and more often men. Exercise mostly works through BMI and health.

46
00:04:40,303 --> 00:04:42,457
So: associations, not causes.

47
00:04:42,457 --> 00:04:46,457
Here is the step that makes the final numbers trustworthy.

48
00:04:46,457 --> 00:04:49,842
We chose the cutoff using only training data:

49
00:04:49,842 --> 00:04:58,457
we refitted the saved model on four folds at a time so every training person got a risk from a model that never saw them,

50
00:04:58,457 --> 00:05:06,457
and those predictions reproduced stage 6 almost exactly. The rule, written before the run: the highest cutoff that still catches 80% of cases.

51
00:05:06,457 --> 00:05:13,226
That came out at 0.153, a predicted risk of about 15%. The calibration was already excellent, so no adjustment.

52
00:05:13,226 --> 00:05:18,764
The cutoff went into a file, and the test command refuses to run without it.

53
00:05:18,764 --> 00:05:27,072
On the right you can see the trade-off: every extra ten points of sensitivity costs roughly 90 to 130 more people flagged per thousand.

54
00:05:27,072 --> 00:05:35,072
Demo, step one: we try to score the test set before any cutoff exists. The command stops with an error and writes nothing.

55
00:05:35,072 --> 00:05:38,764
That lock is what keeps the final numbers honest.

56
00:05:38,764 --> 00:05:45,226
Step two: the threshold command reads only the 202,944 training rows, refits the saved model fold by fold,

57
00:05:45,226 --> 00:05:48,611
and locks the cutoff at 0.1530 in threshold.json.

58
00:05:48,611 --> 00:05:55,073
Step three: with the cutoff saved, the test set is scored once, with 1,000 bootstrap resamples per model.

59
00:05:55,073 --> 00:06:01,535
XGBoost catches 79.4% of cases and flags 37.6% of people; the checklist flags 47.3% for the same cases.

60
00:06:01,535 --> 00:06:07,997
Finally, the committed model and cutoff score two made-up people: a healthy 35-year-old at 1% risk, not flagged,

61
00:06:07,997 --> 00:06:14,766
and a 65-year-old with high blood pressure at 60%, flagged. The full test suite passes: 63 tests, 98% coverage.

62
00:06:14,766 --> 00:06:19,381
And here is the result on the 50,736 people we held back.

63
00:06:19,381 --> 00:06:27,689
At the locked cutoff, the model found 79.4% of people with diabetes or prediabetes, right on the 80% target, with a tight confidence interval.

64
00:06:27,689 --> 00:06:33,535
It flagged 37.6% of people, and one in three of those flagged actually had the condition.

65
00:06:33,535 --> 00:06:39,381
Compared with the paper checklist at its own cutoff, which finds the same share of cases,

66
00:06:39,381 --> 00:06:43,381
that’s 96 fewer false alarms for every thousand people screened.

67
00:06:43,381 --> 00:06:50,766
The test-set scores match what we saw in validation, so nothing was overfitted, and the predicted risks are still well calibrated.

68
00:06:50,766 --> 00:06:56,612
Translated for a clinic: out of every thousand people screened, about 158 have diabetes or prediabetes.

69
00:06:56,612 --> 00:07:06,458
The model sends 376 for a blood test, finds 125 of the 158 and misses 33. The paper checklist finds the same number but sends almost 100 more people.

70
00:07:06,458 --> 00:07:13,227
Logistic regression lands in between, much closer to XGBoost, so the simple model captures about 90% of the gain.

71
00:07:13,227 --> 00:07:21,535
And because the risks are calibrated, a person told their risk is 30% really is in a group where about 30% have the condition.

72
00:07:21,535 --> 00:07:24,920
Now the honest part: who does it miss?

73
00:07:24,920 --> 00:07:33,535
The model leans on BMI, general health, age, blood pressure and cholesterol, so a case who looks ordinary on all five is invisible to it.

74
00:07:33,535 --> 00:07:38,458
The missed cases have the same average BMI as people without the condition.

75
00:07:38,458 --> 00:07:45,843
That shows up in subgroups: under-45s, only 44% of cases are found; at incomes of 50 thousand dollars and up, 68%.

76
00:07:45,843 --> 00:07:51,689
Meanwhile, over-65s and low-income groups get most cases found but also about half of everyone flagged.

77
00:07:51,689 --> 00:07:58,766
And it finds 81% of diagnosed diabetes but only 65% of prediabetes, which is the group screening is really for.

78
00:07:58,766 --> 00:08:02,766
One cutoff for everyone means different trade-offs for different groups.

79
00:08:02,766 --> 00:08:10,766
We also asked the harder question directly: leave out everyone who already has diabetes and look for prediabetes alone. Same pipeline, same rules.

80
00:08:10,766 --> 00:08:15,381
The model still beats the checklist, finding 81% of cases against 70%.

81
00:08:15,381 --> 00:08:22,150
But it has to flag 44% of everyone to do it, and only 4% of those flagged report prediabetes.

82
00:08:22,150 --> 00:08:27,381
That’s the honest headline for early detection from survey answers: it’s possible, but expensive.

83
00:08:27,381 --> 00:08:35,996
One caveat in the model’s favour: most people with prediabetes have never been told, so many of these “false alarms” may really be undiagnosed cases,

84
00:08:35,996 --> 00:08:39,381
and we simply can’t tell from this data.

85
00:08:39,381 --> 00:08:42,766
Six limits we want to be upfront about.

86
00:08:42,766 --> 00:08:49,228
The labels are self-reported at one moment in 2015, so this identifies people who already know their status;

87
00:08:49,228 --> 00:08:57,843
it predicts nothing about who will develop diabetes. 88% of positives already have a diagnosis, so the model partly learns what a diagnosis looks like.

88
00:08:57,843 --> 00:09:03,689
Some of its best answers, like cholesterol checks and blood pressure, partly measure seeing a doctor;

89
00:09:03,689 --> 00:09:10,458
without them it loses a little but still beats the checklist. One cutoff gives different groups very different trade-offs.

90
00:09:10,458 --> 00:09:16,612
The survey extract has no weights and covers one year. And everything here is association, not cause.

91
00:09:16,612 --> 00:09:20,920
So: a research prototype for risk stratification, not a diagnostic tool.

92
00:09:20,920 --> 00:09:30,458
To sum up: with nothing but survey answers, a tuned model can find about eight in ten people with diabetes or prediabetes while sending a fifth fewer people

93
00:09:30,458 --> 00:09:34,150
for testing than the paper checklist clinics use today.

94
00:09:34,150 --> 00:09:40,612
It is weaker for prediabetes and for younger people, and it is a research prototype, not a diagnosis.

95
00:09:40,612 --> 00:09:46,458
Everything is reproducible: one command per stage, and we checked that the data preparation, the split,

96
00:09:46,458 --> 00:09:51,381
the cutoff and the test results rebuild identically, backed by 63 automated tests.

97
00:09:51,381 --> 00:10:00,000
The code, notebooks and reports are all in our repository, and the technical report has the full detail. Thank you, we’re happy to take questions.
